</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn 
<br class="ltx_break">Meta Causal World</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhiyu Zhao, Haoxuan Li, Haifeng Zhang, Jun Wang, Francesco Faccio
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institute of Automation, Chinese Academy of Sciences
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Artificial Intelligence, Chinese Academy of Sciences
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Peking University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span> University College London
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>King Abdullah University of Science and Technology
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The Swiss AI Lab, IDSIA-USI/SUPSI
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jürgen Schmidhuber
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>King Abdullah University of Science and Technology
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The Swiss AI Lab, IDSIA-USI/SUPSI
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mengyue Yang
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corresponding author: mengyue.yang@bristol.ac.uk</span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Bristol
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">When building a world model, a common assumption is that the environment has a single, unchanging underlying causal rule, like applying Newton’s laws to every situation. In reality, what appears as a drifting causal mechanism is often the manifestation of a fixed underlying mechanism seen through a narrow observational window. This brings about a problem that, when building a world model, even subtle shifts in policy or environment states can alter the very observed causal mechanisms.
In this work, we introduce the <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Meta-Causal Graph</span> as world models, a minimal unified representation that efficiently encodes the transformation rules governing how causal structures shift across different latent world states. A single Meta-Causal Graph is composed of multiple causal subgraphs, each triggered by meta state, which is in the latent state space. Building on this representation, we introduce a <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Causality-Seeking Agent</span> whose objectives are to (1) identify the meta states that trigger each subgraph, (2) discover the corresponding causal relationships by agent curiosity-driven intervention policy, and (3) iteratively refine the Meta-Causal Graph through ongoing curiosity-driven exploration and agent experiences. Experiments on both synthetic tasks and a challenging robot arm manipulation task demonstrate that our method robustly captures shifts in causal dynamics and generalizes effectively to previously unseen contexts.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">World models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>)</cite> have emerged as a critical component in reinforcement learning, enabling agents to simulate and plan in complex environments. These models aim to capture the underlying dynamics of the environment, allowing agents to predict future states and evaluate potential actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>)</cite>. However, simply learning which variables correlate can mislead an agent when the world’s dynamics change. Recently, causality has been adopted to improve world models for interactive and complex environments, since causal rules—describing how one factor brings about another—capture the underlying data-generation process and yield more robust and generalizable decision-making <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>. While many existing approaches incorporate notions of causality, they typically fail to capture the open-world causal relationships.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.23068v1/curious_intro.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/109;" width="476" height="109" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of the Meta-Causal Graph concept and the Curious Causality-Seeking Agent framework. <span id="S1.F1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ground Truth</span>: The causal relationship between pushing and opening depends on the latent state (locked vs. unlocked). <span id="S1.F1.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations of Existing Approaches</span>: Problem 1: Single uniform causal graphs fail to capture context-dependent variations in causal relationships; Problem 2: Domain modeling requires a priori knowledge of state labels, limiting generalization to novel contexts. <span id="S1.F1.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our Approach</span>: Curious Causality-Seeking Agents actively intervene to verify causal relationships and discover the critical meta states that determine when causal structures change, enabling the agent to build a comprehensive Meta-Causal Graph without requiring predefined domain labels.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To identify the underlying world rules, traditional causal models rely predominantly on observational data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>)</cite>, implicitly assuming a fixed causal structure. In open-ended environments, although the ground-truth causal laws remain unchanged, the observational data is collected through local, context-limited exploration. Under conditions of partial observability, this invariance becomes obscured within a limited observational window, giving rise to the illusion of shifting causal relationships—a rule valid in one context may break down in another. For instance, the "push <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> open" relation holds for an unlocked door, but this relation vanishes when the door is locked. In this case, the same action produces no effect, leaving the true causal mechanism ambiguous. Traditional causal modeling in such scenarios faces two main challenges: (1) methods that learn a single uniform (time series-based) causal graph neglect these context-dependent changes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>)</cite>, and (2) lack of an active curiosity-driven exploration strategy, which makes it struggle to adapt to discover the global causal rules in rich, unseen variations of potential open-ended worlds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To deal with these two challenges, we propose the <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Meta-Causal Graph</span> to describe the world, which is a unified structure that captures how causal relationships evolve across different states. Rather than relying on a uniform graph, the Meta-Causal Graph is a minimal representation that contains multiple causal subgraphs, each corresponding to a particular meta state. Transitions between these meta states “trigger” the activation of the appropriate subgraph, allowing us to model shifts in causal influence, such as when a locked door severs the "push <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> open" link. We introduce a framework termed <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Curious Causality-Seeking Agent</span>, which actively explores interventions to uncover the Meta-Causal Graph. The main contributions of this work are given below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Theoretically, we derive sufficient conditions ensuring that strategically chosen families of interventions uniquely identify meta-causal graphs, overcoming observational limitations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Methodologically, we design a framework which leverages a curiosity-driven exploration strategy where agents selectively intervene in the environment to learn a causal world model. First, we employ interventional verification to directly test whether specific variables causally influence others under different state conditions. Unlike methods relying solely on observational data, our approach actively intervenes on variables to establish causal links. Second, we introduce a targeted exploration strategy designed to discover meta states, critical configurations where causal structure changes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Empirically, our approach demonstrates substantial improvements over purely observational baselines in simulated benchmarks, showcasing its capability to accurately recover complex causal structures. By explicitly modeling and reasoning about these causal relationships through active interventions, our proposed method significantly enhances the robustness and adaptability of world models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we employ causal modeling to construct world models. We begin by introducing fundamental definitions and terminology related to causal graphs and interventions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Causal graph</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A causal subgraph is formalized as a directed acyclic graph (DAG) <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=([p],E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=([p],E)</annotation></semantics></math>, where the vertex set <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]=\{1,2,\dots,p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]=\{1,2,\dots,p\}</annotation></semantics></math> indexes a collection of random variables <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{X_{i}\}_{i=1}^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{X_{i}\}_{i=1}^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> with joint density <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(X)</annotation></semantics></math>. The edge set <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\subseteq[p]\times[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\subseteq[p]\times[p]</annotation></semantics></math> encodes direct causal relationships between variables. For each node <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[p]</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Pa}_{\mathcal{G}}(i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pa</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Pa}_{\mathcal{G}}(i)</annotation></semantics></math> for its set of parents. An edge <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\to j\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\to j\in E</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> exerts a direct causal influence on <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Intervention</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Directly learning the causal structure from observational data is often insufficient, as it can lead to multiple possible causal graphs that share the same v-structures. To resolve this ambiguity, we can use interventions to break the observational ambiguity.
Intervention is a powerful tool in causality. An intervention refers to the manipulation of a variable within a causal model, often represented by the do-operator. For example, consider intervening on state <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the system originally takes the value <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}=x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}=x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. An intervention is performed by setting the state variable to a new value, as in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{do}(X_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>do</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{do}(X_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, such as setting “door open” to true in the environment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">During world-model learning, we perform a sequence of environment interventions to reveal causal structure. To better formalize the intervention influence, in this paper, we introduce the notion of an <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">intervention target</span>. At intervention step <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we select a single variable <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as the target, which we denote by the intervention target <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k}=\{i\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k}=\{i\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in[p]</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying an intervention <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(X_{I_{k}}=x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(X_{I_{k}}=x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> removes all incoming causal links into <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Concretely, if <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is the original DAG, the <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">intervention graph</em> <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by removing every edge of the form <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\to i,\quad\forall\,j\in\mathrm{Pa}_{\mathcal{G}}(i)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Pa</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\to i,\quad\forall\,j\in\mathrm{Pa}_{\mathcal{G}}(i),</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. This modified graph accurately reflects the altered generative process under the do-operation on <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Intervention graph <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>)</cite>)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=([p],E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=([p],E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a DAG with vertex set <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[p]</annotation></semantics></math> and edge set <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subset[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subset[p]</annotation></semantics></math> an intervention target. The intervention graph of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the DAG <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{(I)}=([p],E^{(I)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{(I)}=([p],E^{(I)})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{(I)}:=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{(I)}:=</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{a\to b\mid a\to b\in E,b\notin I\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a\to b\mid a\to b\in E,b\notin I\}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interventional Markov Equivalence</span> is defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Interventional Markov Equivalence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>)</cite>)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Two DAGs <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are interventional Markov equivalent given a set of intervention target <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same skeleton and the same v-structures, and <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{(I)}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{(I)}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{(I)}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{(I)}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> have the same skeleton and the same v-structures for all <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Then the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interventional Markov Equivalence Class (I-MEC)</span> is the set of DAGs that are interventional Markov equivalent to each other given a set of intervention targets <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a set of intervention targets <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can determine the I-MEC of the system. However, the DAGs in the I-MEC are not unique. We denote the set of DAGs in the I-MEC as <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although DAGs in <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> share the same skeleton and v-structures, they may contain edges with different directions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we present our Meta-Causal Graph for a world formulation. We begin by defining the Meta-Causal Graph, which is a compact causal graph comprising multiple context-specific causal subgraphs, varying from latent meta states in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Meta-Causal Graph ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. Then, we show the identifiability of meta states in Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and their indicated causal subgraphs in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Identification of Causal Subgraphs ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> by interventions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Meta-Causal Graph</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider an environment whose states are represented by a set of variables <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{X_{i}\}_{i\in[p]}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{X_{i}\}_{i\in[p]}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the “door open” is a state in the environment. <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in the environment can be intervened on by manipulating its values directly or influenced by taking actions indirectly. Then, the Meta-Causal Graph is defined on the environment state as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Meta-Causal Graph)<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Meta-Causal Graph <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{MG}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{MG}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a collection of causal subgraphs
<math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{MG}\;=\;\{\mathcal{G}_{u}\}_{u\in U}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{MG}\;=\;\{\mathcal{G}_{u}\}_{u\in U},</annotation></semantics></math>
where each <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a distinct meta state <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math>. The causality skeleton matrix <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is a binary matrix where <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u}[i,j]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u}[i,j]=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that variable <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is a parent of variable <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in Pa(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in Pa(j)</annotation></semantics></math>) in the causal graph <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. A state-to-meta state mapping
<math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:\mathcal{X}\;\to\;U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:\mathcal{X}\;\to\;U</annotation></semantics></math>
assigns each system observation <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to its active meta state <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u=C(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u=C(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. We call <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{MG}=(\{\mathcal{G}_{u}\},C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{MG}=(\{\mathcal{G}_{u}\},C)</annotation></semantics></math> a Meta-Causal Graph if the following conditions are satisfied:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a ground truth mapping </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:\mathcal{X}\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:\mathcal{X}\to U</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> determining the real meta states.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Causality skeleton
matrices of causal subgraphs are sufficiently different. For all </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\neq u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\neq u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u}\neq M_{u^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u}\neq M_{u^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, i.e., there exists an index </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> such that </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u}[i,j]\neq M_{u^{\prime}}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u}[i,j]\neq M_{u^{\prime}}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The underlying Meta-Causal Graph in the environment is assumed to be invariant; however, its complete identification requires two steps: (1) identification of latent meta states, and (2) identification of the causal subgraph corresponding to each meta state.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Identifiability of Meta States</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we will discuss the identifiability of the meta states from the environment.
We first introduce the concept of <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">swap-label equivalence</span> between two mappings.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Swap-Label Equivalence)<span id="Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Two mappings <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}:\mathcal{X}\to U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}:\mathcal{X}\to U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}:\mathcal{X}\to U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}:\mathcal{X}\to U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are swap-label equivalent if there exists a permutation <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:U_{1}\to U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:U_{1}\to U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}(x)=g(C_{1}(x)),\forall x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}(x)=g(C_{1}(x)),\forall x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The definition indicates that if we can identify the meta states up to an permutation transformation, we say that the meta states are identifiable up to label swapping.
We then show that the meta states are identifiable up to swap-label equivalence under the following assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Mixed Data Structure Learning)<span id="Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{MG}=\{\mathcal{G}_{i}\}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{MG}=\{\mathcal{G}_{i}\}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Meta-Causal Graph with <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> causal subgraphs corresponding to distinct meta states <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider a dataset <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> where each sample is generated from one of these causal subgraphs. Let <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathcal{D}}\subseteq\{1,2,...,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathcal{D}}\subseteq\{1,2,...,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the indices of causal subgraphs that actually contributed samples to <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. When learning a single causal graph <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the mixed dataset <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> (treating all samples as if they were generated from a single causal subgraph), the estimated parent set for each variable <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Pa_{\hat{\mathcal{G}}}(X_{j})=\bigcup_{i\in S_{\mathcal{D}}}Pa_{\mathcal{G}_{i}}(X_{j})\quad\forall j\in[p]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Pa_{\hat{\mathcal{G}}}(X_{j})=\bigcup_{i\in S_{\mathcal{D}}}Pa_{\mathcal{G}_{i}}(X_{j})\quad\forall j\in[p].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Under this assumption, the unified graph from pooled data contains the union of all direct parent–child edges present in each causal subgraph. Consequently, the latent meta state are identifiable as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Identifiability of Meta States)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the learned mapping <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}:\mathcal{X}\to\hat{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}:\mathcal{X}\to\hat{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is swap-label equivalent to the ground truth mapping <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:\mathcal{X}\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:\mathcal{X}\to U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Identifiability when number of meta states is unknown: </span>In practice, we often overparameterize the meta state space by choosing more clusters than actually exist. We prove that this overparameterization does not harm identifiability: even with excess clusters, each true causal subgraph can still be recovered. The following theorem shows that, provided the number of clusters is sufficiently large, the underlying subgraph structures are identifiable up to the observational equivalence.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Two mappings <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}:\mathcal{X}\to U_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}:\mathcal{X}\to U_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}:\mathcal{X}\to U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}:\mathcal{X}\to U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are observationally equivalent if there exists a mapping <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:U_{1}\to U_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:U_{1}\to U_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}(x)=\phi(C_{1}(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}(x)=\phi(C_{1}(x))</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Identifiability of Overparameterized Meta States)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,
if the learned mapping <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}:\mathcal{X}\to\hat{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}:\mathcal{X}\to\hat{U}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\hat{U}|&gt;|U|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\hat{U}|&gt;|U|</annotation></semantics></math>, the learned mapping <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is observationally equivalent to the ground truth mapping <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:\mathcal{X}\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:\mathcal{X}\to U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Identification of Causal Subgraphs</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we demonstrate that causal subgraphs for each meta state become uniquely identifiable once edge directionality is determined through interventions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1 </span>Causal Subgraph Identification</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ multiple interventions to determine the causal structure of environment, represented as <span id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">intervention target indicator set</span> <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}=\{I_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}=\{I_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{k}\subset\{[p]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{k}\subset\{[p]\}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises the indices of intervened variables selected from state variable set <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{X_{i}\}_{i\in[p]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{X_{i}\}_{i\in[p]}</annotation></semantics></math> at intervention step <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. These intervention sets are collected from historical data or agent interactions. We establish sufficient conditions ensuring causal graph edge identifiability through appropriate interventional selection.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Identifiability of Causal Subgraph)<span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The causal subgraph <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> can be uniquely identified if there exists an intervention target indicator set <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all edges <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\to b\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\to b\in D</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an intervention target <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|I\cap\{a,b\}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|I\cap\{a,b\}|=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This proposition establishes that causal subgraph identifiability requires edge-specific interventions targeting single connected variables, enabling directional determination.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Curious Causality-Seeking Agents for Meta Causal World Modeling</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a novel framework to learn the Meta-Causal Graph by a Causality-Seeking Agent. Our approach consists of three main components: (1) Meta-Causal Graph discovery from agent experience, (2) curiosity-driven interventional exploration for seeking Meta-Causal Graph in an unknown world and (3) complete world model learning and updating. In this section, we detail each component and describe how they work together to enable discovery of causal structures.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Meta-Causal Graph Learning from Experience</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We learn causal subgraphs under latent meta states from agent experience. Specifically, the process contains two steps: (1) identifying the latent meta state, which activates a specific causal subgraph, and (2) learning the subgraph from the agent’s explored experience data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Identifying Latent Meta State:</span> We embed the observed state and assign it to a corresponding meta state <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math> by vector quantization as follows. We define the meta state assignment as <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C(x)\;=\;\arg\min_{u\in U}\bigl\lVert E(x)-e_{u}\bigr\rVert_{2}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mi>arg</mi><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(x)\;=\;\arg\min_{u\in U}\bigl\lVert E(x)-e_{u}\bigr\rVert_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\colon\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\colon\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is an encoder mapping the observed state <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to a <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional embedding, and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{u}\}_{u\in U}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{u}\}_{u\in U}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable codebook of prototype embeddings, each representing a distinct meta state.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subgraph Learning:</span> After obtaining the embedding <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> for a given state, we employ a decoder network
<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D:\mathbb{R}^{d}\;\to\;[0,1]^{p\times p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.558em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D:\mathbb{R}^{d}\;\to\;[0,1]^{p\times p}</annotation></semantics></math>
to predict a probability matrix <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>, which estimates the underlying causal skeleton matrix <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each entry <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}_{u}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}_{u}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the probability that the directed edge <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\to j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\to j</annotation></semantics></math> is present in meta state <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>. We then sample each <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Bernoulli}(\hat{M}_{u}[i,j])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bernoulli</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Bernoulli}(\hat{M}_{u}[i,j])</annotation></semantics></math> using the Gumbel-Softmax reparameterization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>)</cite>, enabling end-to-end gradient-based learning of the discrete structure.
To update the codebook, we use the following update rule:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{quantization}}=\|\text{sg}(E_{\phi}(X))-e_{u}\|_{2}^{2}+\beta\|\text{sg}(e_{u})-e_{u}\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>quantization</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{quantization}}=\|\text{sg}(E_{\phi}(X))-e_{u}\|_{2}^{2}+\beta\|\text{sg}(e_{u})-e_{u}\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sg}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sg}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the stop-gradient operator. The first term is the reconstruction loss and the second term is the commitment loss to avoid the output of the encoder growing arbitrarily <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Curious Reward of Interventional Exploration for Seeking Causality in Unknown World</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Accurate identification of the Meta-Causal Graph cannot rely solely on passive agent experience. First, causal structures inferred from observational trajectories alone may misorient edges. Second, an agent’s experiences cover only a subset of possible world states, leaving portions of the causal graph unobserved. To efficiently explore the state space and discover transitions between different meta states, we design a curiosity-driven reward function:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\text{do}(X_{i}^{t}=x^{\prime}_{i}))=\sum_{i,j\in[p]}H(\hat{M}_{C(X^{t})[i,j]})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>do</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\text{do}(X_{i}^{t}=x^{\prime}_{i}))=\sum_{i,j\in[p]}H(\hat{M}_{C(X^{t})[i,j]}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the Shannon entropy of the posterior distribution over potential causal relationships given the current state <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This reward function encourages the agent to explore states in which the underlying causal structure remains uncertain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To validate and refine the learned causal structures, we employ interventional verification to directly test whether specific variables causally influence others under different state conditions.
For each variable <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in our system, we perform interventions denoted as <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{do}(X_{i}=x^{\prime}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>do</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{do}(X_{i}=x^{\prime}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where we intervene <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by setting it to a specific value <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and observe the consequent changes in other variables. The causal effect of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is then estimated as:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{ij}=\log P(X_{j}^{t+1}|X^{t},\text{do}(X_{i}=x^{\prime}_{i}))-\log P(X_{j}^{t+1}|X^{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>do</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{ij}=\log P(X_{j}^{t+1}|X^{t},\text{do}(X_{i}=x^{\prime}_{i}))-\log P(X_{j}^{t+1}|X^{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is the difference in log probability between the intervened and the observational distribution of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We incorporate the causal effect estimation into the objective function as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{mask}}=-\lambda_{1}\sum_{\{(i,j)|\Delta_{ij}&gt;0\}}\log\hat{M}_{ij}+\lambda_{2}\sum_{\{(i,j)|\Delta_{ij}&lt;0\}}\log(\hat{M}_{ij})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>mask</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mi>log</mi><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></munder><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{mask}}=-\lambda_{1}\sum_{\{(i,j)|\Delta_{ij}&gt;0\}}\log\hat{M}_{ij}+\lambda_{2}\sum_{\{(i,j)|\Delta_{ij}&lt;0\}}\log(\hat{M}_{ij}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are hyperparameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Transition Probability Learning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The previous subsections describe how to identify the parent set of each state variable, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Pa_{\mathcal{G}_{u}}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Pa_{\mathcal{G}_{u}}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, using the learned causal skeleton matrix <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this section, we demonstrate how to leverage the confirmed causal skeleton to quantify inter-variable causal effects, thereby grounding and validating the transition probabilities of the world model for next-state prediction. The model is optimized via a maximum likelihood estimation objective:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{MLE}}=-\log P(X_{j}^{t+1}|Pa_{\mathcal{G}_{u}}(X_{j}))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MLE</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{MLE}}=-\log P(X_{j}^{t+1}|Pa_{\mathcal{G}_{u}}(X_{j})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The complete world model learning objective.</span> To encourage the discovery of parsimonious causal structures that capture essential relationships while avoiding spurious connections, we incorporate a sparsity regularization term: <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{sparse}}=\|\hat{M}_{\theta}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>sparse</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{sparse}}=\|\hat{M}_{\theta}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\hat{M}_{\theta}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\hat{M}_{\theta}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the L1 norm of the causal skeleton probability matrix, promoting sparsity in the learned causal graphs.
The complete optimization objective integrates multiple components is as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{MLE}}+\lambda_{\text{sparse}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{sparse}}+\lambda_{\text{mask}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{mask}}+\lambda_{\text{quantization}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{quantization}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>MLE</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>sparse</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>sparse</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>mask</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>mask</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>quantization</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>quantization</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{MLE}}+\lambda_{\text{sparse}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{sparse}}+\lambda_{\text{mask}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{mask}}+\lambda_{\text{quantization}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{quantization}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Training proceeds by alternating among interventional exploration, causal subgraph updating, world model learning, and codebook refinement. Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.3 Transition Probability Learning ‣ 4 Curious Causality-Seeking Agents for Meta Causal World Modeling ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the complete learning procedure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Prediction accuracy on OOD states in the Chemical environment. The number of noisy nodes in the downstream tasks is denoted as <math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. All values are reported in percentage (%).</span></figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:102.7pt;vertical-align:-49.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-113.0pt,33.7pt) scale(0.604101449218053,0.604101449218053) ;">
<table id="S4.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Algorithm</span></th>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">Fork</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="3">Chain</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">n=2</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">n=4</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">n=6</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">n=2</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">n=4</td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">n=6</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.5.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MCG (ours)</span></th>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">63.18<math id="S4.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>13.94</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">50.47<math id="S4.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>9.87</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.5.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T1.5.1.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">50.04<math id="S4.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>8.56</span></td>
