<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Theoretical Analysis and Discussion</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section establishes the theoretical foundations for identifying meta states and their corresponding causal subgraphs within our proposed framework. We present a comprehensive analysis of identifiability conditions under two distinct scenarios: swap-label equivalence and observational equivalence. The former addresses cases where meta states can be permuted without altering the underlying causal structure, while the latter examines situations involving overparameterization where the learned model contains more meta states than the ground truth. Additionally, we introduce intervention reachability to formalize which causal relationships are identifiable via feasible interventions.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Summary of Key Results</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our theoretical analysis yields key results on the identifiability of meta states and causal structures:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Swap-Label Equivalence:</span> Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we demonstrate that the learned mapping function is swap-label equivalent to the ground truth mapping (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Identifiability of Meta States). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). This equivalence ensures that while the specific labels assigned to meta states may differ between learned and ground truth mappings, the underlying causal relationships remain preserved. The proof demonstrates that any departure from this equivalence leads to higher L1 norm of causal skeleton matrices and thus contradicts the optimality principle underlying our framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observational Equivalence:</span> For overparameterized scenarios where the learned model contains more meta states than the ground truth, we prove that observational equivalence is maintained (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Identifiability of Overparameterized Meta States). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). This result demonstrates that introducing additional meta states does not compromise the recovery of true causal relationships, since the learned mapping consistently preserves the same causal skeleton matrices across all states.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intervention Reachability:</span> We formalize the mathematical framework for determining which interventions are feasible given environmental constraints. Through matrix representations of state transitions and intervention capabilities, we establish conditions under which specific causal relationships can be identified through sequential interventions (Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Reachability Analysis). ‣ A.8 Reachability of Interventions ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis and Discussion ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). This analysis provides practical guidance for experimental design in causal discovery.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structural Assumptions:</span> Our results rely on Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which posits that learning from mixed datasets yields causal graphs encompassing the union of all contributing causal relationships. This assumption is both theoretically justified and practically reasonable, as it ensures comprehensive coverage of causal dependencies present in heterogeneous data sources.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The theoretical framework presented in this section provides rigorous guarantees for the identifiability of causal structures while accommodating practical constraints inherent in real-world applications, thereby establishing the soundness of our proposed methodology.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Swap-Label Equivalence</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of Meta-Causal Graphs, swap-label equivalence captures the invariance of causal structure under permutation of meta state labels. This phenomenon arises when different labelings of meta states yield identical causal relationships across the state space.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a Meta-Causal Graph <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with meta states <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{1},u_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{1},u_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding causal subgraphs <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{M_{u_{1}},M_{u_{2}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{M_{u_{1}},M_{u_{2}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#A1.F6.sf1" title="In Figure 6 ‣ A.2 Swap-Label Equivalence ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis and Discussion ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(a)</span></a>. An alternative Meta-Causal Graph <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with meta states <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{u}_{1},\hat{u}_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{u}_{1},\hat{u}_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and subgraphs <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{1}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{2}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{1}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{2}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is shown in Figure <a href="#A1.F6.sf2" title="In Figure 6 ‣ A.2 Swap-Label Equivalence ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis and Discussion ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6(b)</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Two mappings <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:\mathcal{X}\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:\mathcal{X}\to U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}:\mathcal{X}\to\hat{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}:\mathcal{X}\to\hat{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are swap-label equivalent if there exists a bijection <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:U\to\hat{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:U\to\hat{U}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any state <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, the causal skeleton matrices satisfy:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{C(x)}=\hat{M}_{g(C(x))}=\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{C(x)}=\hat{M}_{g(C(x))}=\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This equivalence ensures that the learned mapping <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> preserves the same causal structure as the ground truth mapping <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, despite potentially different meta state assignments. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.2 Swap-Label Equivalence ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis and Discussion ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, such permutations preserve the fundamental causal relationships within the Meta-Causal Graph.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F6.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/label_swap_partition_1.svg" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/303;" width="429" height="303"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F6.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/label_swap_partition_2.svg" id="A1.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/303;" width="429" height="303"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/label_swap_1.svg" id="A1.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:429/607;" width="429" height="607"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F6.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A1.F6.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Meta-Causal Graph with two meta states <math id="A1.F6.sf1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.F6.sf1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding causal subgraph <math id="A1.F6.sf1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.F6.sf1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/label_swap_2.svg" id="A1.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:429/607;" width="429" height="607"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F6.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A1.F6.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Meta-Causal Graph with two meta states <math id="A1.F6.sf2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.F6.sf2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding causal subgraph <math id="A1.F6.sf2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.580em">^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.F6.sf2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.580em">^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A1.F6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Swap-label equivalence in Meta-Causal Graphs.<span id="A1.F6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Left: Ground truth Meta-Causal Graph with meta states <math id="A1.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{1},u_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{1},u_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding causal subgraphs <math id="A1.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{M_{u_{1}},M_{u_{2}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{M_{u_{1}},M_{u_{2}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Right: Learned Meta-Causal Graph with meta states <math id="A1.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{u}_{1},\hat{u}_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{u}_{1},\hat{u}_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and subgraphs <math id="A1.F6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{1}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{2}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.580em">^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.580em">^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{1}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{2}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Despite different meta state labels, both mappings produce identical causal skeleton matrices for any state <math id="A1.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating structural equivalence under label permutation.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Identifiability of Meta States). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the reconstruction objective for learning causal skeleton matrices from mixed data. For any clustering <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}:\mathcal{X}\to\hat{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}:\mathcal{X}\to\hat{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the learned skeleton matrix for meta state <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. Under Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, this matrix satisfies:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}[i,j]=\mathbb{I}\left[\bigcup_{x:\hat{C}(x)=\hat{u}}M_{C(x)}[i,j]=1\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}[i,j]=\mathbb{I}\left[\bigcup_{x:\hat{C}(x)=\hat{u}}M_{C(x)}[i,j]=1\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now consider two cases:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1:<span id="A1.SS3.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> perfectly aligns with <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> up to label permutation. Then there exists a bijection <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:U\to\hat{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:U\to\hat{U}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}(x)=g(C(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}(x)=g(C(x))</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}_{g(u)}=M_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}_{g(u)}=M_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math>, achieving perfect reconstruction of individual causal structures.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:<span id="A1.SS3.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> merges states from different true meta states. Then there exist <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},x_{2}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},x_{2}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(x_{1})=u_{1}\neq u_{2}=C(x_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(x_{1})=u_{1}\neq u_{2}=C(x_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> but <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}(x_{1})=\hat{C}(x_{2})=\hat{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}(x_{1})=\hat{C}(x_{2})=\hat{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</span></span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}=M_{u_{1}}\cup M_{u_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}=M_{u_{1}}\cup M_{u_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cup" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∪</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cup</annotation></semantics></math> denotes element-wise logical OR. Since <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u_{1}}\neq M_{u_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u_{1}}\neq M_{u_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\neq M_{u_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\neq M_{u_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\neq M_{u_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\neq M_{u_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the L1 norm. For binary matrices, <math id="A1.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|M\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|M\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> equals the number of edges in the corresponding causal graph. Since the union operation can only add edges (never remove them), we have:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}=\|M_{u_{1}}\cup M_{u_{2}}\|_{1}\geq\max\{\|M_{u_{1}}\|_{1},\|M_{u_{2}}\|_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}=\|M_{u_{1}}\cup M_{u_{2}}\|_{1}\geq\max\{\|M_{u_{1}}\|_{1},\|M_{u_{2}}\|_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Moreover, since <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u_{1}}\neq M_{u_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u_{1}}\neq M_{u_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists at least one position <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u_{1}}[i,j]\neq M_{u_{2}}[i,j]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u_{1}}[i,j]\neq M_{u_{2}}[i,j]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}&gt;\|M_{u_{1}}\|_{1}\quad\text{and}\quad\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}&gt;\|M_{u_{2}}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}&gt;\|M_{u_{1}}\|_{1}\quad\text{and}\quad\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}&gt;\|M_{u_{2}}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider the expected L1 norm under each partition. For the true partition <math id="A1.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{X}}[\|M_{C(x)}\|_{1}]=\sum_{u\in U}P(C(x)=u)\cdot\|M_{u}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{X}}[\|M_{C(x)}\|_{1}]=\sum_{u\in U}P(C(x)=u)\cdot\|M_{u}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the suboptimal partition <math id="A1.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> that merges distinct meta states:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{X}}[\|\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}\|_{1}]=\sum_{\hat{u}\in\hat{U}}P(\hat{C}(x)=\hat{u})\cdot\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{X}}[\|\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}\|_{1}]=\sum_{\hat{u}\in\hat{U}}P(\hat{C}(x)=\hat{u})\cdot\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since merging increases the L1 norm (as shown above), and each merged cluster <math id="A1.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="A1.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}&gt;\|M_{u}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}&gt;\|M_{u}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for the constituent true meta states <math id="A1.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{X}}[\|\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}\|_{1}]&gt;\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{X}}[\|M_{C(x)}\|_{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{X}}[\|\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}\|_{1}]&gt;\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{X}}[\|M_{C(x)}\|_{1}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Observational Equivalence</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Observational equivalence captures scenarios where learned meta state mappings preserve causal structure despite overparameterization. Formally, two mappings <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:\mathcal{X}\to U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:\mathcal{X}\to U</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}:\mathcal{X}\to\hat{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}:\mathcal{X}\to\hat{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are observationally equivalent if for every state <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="A1.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{C(x)}=\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{C(x)}=\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{C(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{C(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> are the causal skeleton matrices corresponding to the assigned meta states.
This equivalence ensures that the learned mapping <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}</annotation></semantics></math> captures identical causal relationships as the ground truth mapping <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, even when the learned meta state space <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{U}</annotation></semantics></math> is larger than the true space <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. Overparameterization may result in redundant meta states, but the essential causal structure remains preserved.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ A.4 Observational Equivalence ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis and Discussion ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> illustrates this concept. Left: Ground truth Meta-Causal Graph with meta states <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{1},u_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{1},u_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and subgraphs <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{M_{u_{1}},M_{u_{2}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{M_{u_{1}},M_{u_{2}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Right: Learned overparameterized Meta-Causal Graph with three meta states <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{u}_{1},\hat{u}_{2},\hat{u}_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{u}_{1},\hat{u}_{2},\hat{u}_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and corresponding subgraphs <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{1}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{2}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{3}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{1}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{2}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{3}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Despite having an additional meta state, both mappings yield identical causal skeleton matrices for any given state <math id="A1.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating observational equivalence under overparameterization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F7.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/x1.svg" id="A1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/269;" width="381" height="269"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F7.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/observational_equivalence_partition.svg" id="A1.F7.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/269;" width="381" height="269"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/x2.svg" id="A1.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/674;" width="476" height="674"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A1.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Meta-Causal Graph with two meta states.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/observational_equivalence.svg" id="A1.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_align_right ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/336;" width="476" height="336"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A1.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Meta-Causal Graph with three meta states.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A1.F7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Observational equivalence under overparameterization.<span id="A1.F7.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Left: Ground truth Meta-Causal Graph with meta states <math id="A1.F7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{1},u_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{1},u_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and causal subgraphs <math id="A1.F7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{M_{u_{1}},M_{u_{2}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{M_{u_{1}},M_{u_{2}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Right: Overparameterized Meta-Causal Graph with three meta states <math id="A1.F7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{u}_{1},\hat{u}_{2},\hat{u}_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{u}_{1},\hat{u}_{2},\hat{u}_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and subgraphs <math id="A1.F7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{1}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{2}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{3}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.580em">^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.580em">^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">^</mo></mover><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo mathsize="1.580em">^</mo></mover><mn>3</mn></msub></msub><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{1}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{2}},\hat{M}_{\hat{u}_{3}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Despite the additional meta state, both mappings produce identical causal skeleton matrices for any state <math id="A1.F7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating that overparameterization preserves causal structure.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Identifiability of Overparameterized Meta States). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove that despite overparameterization, the learned mapping preserves the causal structure of the ground truth mapping.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{U}_{\text{active}}=\{\hat{u}\in\hat{U}:\exists x\in\mathcal{X}\text{ s.t. }\hat{C}(x)=\hat{u}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>active</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> s.t. </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{U}_{\text{active}}=\{\hat{u}\in\hat{U}:\exists x\in\mathcal{X}\text{ s.t. }\hat{C}(x)=\hat{u}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of meta states that are actually assigned to some state in <math id="A1.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1:<span id="A1.SS5.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We first establish that the learned mapping cannot merge distinct true meta states. Suppose for contradiction that there exist <math id="A1.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},x_{2}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},x_{2}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:</span></span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C(x_{1})=u_{1}\neq u_{2}=C(x_{2})\quad\text{but}\quad\hat{C}(x_{1})=\hat{C}(x_{2})=\hat{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>but</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C(x_{1})=u_{1}\neq u_{2}=C(x_{2})\quad\text{but}\quad\hat{C}(x_{1})=\hat{C}(x_{2})=\hat{u}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the learned causal skeleton matrix would be:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}=M_{u_{1}}\cup M_{u_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}=M_{u_{1}}\cup M_{u_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A1.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u_{1}}\neq M_{u_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u_{1}}\neq M_{u_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A1.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}&gt;\max\{\|M_{u_{1}}\|_{1},\|M_{u_{2}}\|_{1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}\|_{1}&gt;\max\{\|M_{u_{1}}\|_{1},\|M_{u_{2}}\|_{1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, increasing the expected structural complexity as shown in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Identifiability of Meta States). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. This contradicts optimal clustering.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2:<span id="A1.SS5.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Since distinct true meta states cannot be merged, each active learned meta state <math id="A1.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}\in\hat{U}_{\text{active}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>active</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}\in\hat{U}_{\text{active}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to exactly one true meta state. That is, for each <math id="A1.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}\in\hat{U}_{\text{active}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>U</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>active</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}\in\hat{U}_{\text{active}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a unique <math id="A1.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math> such that:
<math id="A1.SS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x:\hat{C}(x)=\hat{u}\}\subseteq\{x:C(x)=u\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x:\hat{C}(x)=\hat{u}\}\subseteq\{x:C(x)=u\}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3:<span id="A1.SS5.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> For any <math id="A1.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="A1.SS5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{C}(x)=\hat{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{C}(x)=\hat{u}</annotation></semantics></math> and the corresponding true meta state is <math id="A1.SS5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, then by Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</span></span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}=M_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{M}_{\hat{u}}=M_{u}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Therefore, <math id="A1.SS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{C(x)}=\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{C(x)}=\hat{M}_{\hat{C}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SS5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, establishing observational equivalence.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/ground_truth_partition.svg" id="A1.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/336;" width="476" height="336"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A1.F8.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ground truth graph.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/ground_truth_matrix.svg" id="A1.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/336;" width="476" height="336"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A1.F8.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Causal skeleton matrix of the ground truth graph.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F8.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/overparamterization_partition.svg" id="A1.F8.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/336;" width="476" height="336"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A1.F8.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Learned graph with overparameterization.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F8.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/overparamterization_matrix.svg" id="A1.F8.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/336;" width="476" height="336"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A1.F8.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Causal skeleton matrix of the learned graph.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A1.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of the Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Identifiability of Overparameterized Meta States). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The upper figure shows the ground truth graph and its causal skeleton matrix. The lower figure shows the learned graph with overparameterization and its causal skeleton matrix.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.6 </span>Disscussion on Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{MG}=\{\mathcal{G}_{i}\}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{MG}=\{\mathcal{G}_{i}\}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be a Meta-Causal Graph with <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> causal subgraphs corresponding to distinct meta states <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in U</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider a dataset <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> where each sample is generated from one of these causal subgraphs. Let <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\mathcal{D}}\subseteq\{1,2,...,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\mathcal{D}}\subseteq\{1,2,...,K\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the indices of causal subgraphs that actually contributed samples to <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. When learning a single causal graph <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the mixed dataset <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> (treating all samples as if they were generated from a single causal subgraph), the estimated parent set for each variable <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Pa_{\hat{\mathcal{G}}}(X_{j})=\bigcup_{i\in S_{\mathcal{D}}}Pa_{\mathcal{G}_{i}}(X_{j})\quad\forall j\in[p]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Pa_{\hat{\mathcal{G}}}(X_{j})=\bigcup_{i\in S_{\mathcal{D}}}Pa_{\mathcal{G}_{i}}(X_{j})\quad\forall j\in[p].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> states that learning from mixed data generated by multiple causal subgraphs yields a unified graph capturing the union of all parent-child relationships across contributing subgraphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A1.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ A.6 Disscussion on Assumption ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis and Discussion ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> illustrates this assumption. The left panel shows the causal skeleton matrices <math id="A1.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> of individual subgraphs that contribute data to <math id="A1.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. The right panel shows the learned causal skeleton matrix <math id="A1.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{M}</annotation></semantics></math> from the pooled dataset. Red elements highlight the union property where edges present in any contributing subgraph appear in the learned graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Mathematically, this relationship can be expressed as:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{M}[i,j]=\mathbb{I}\left[\bigcup_{u\in S_{\mathcal{D}}}M_{u}[i,j]=1\right]\quad\forall i,j\in[p]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.167em">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{M}[i,j]=\mathbb{I}\left[\bigcup_{u\in S_{\mathcal{D}}}M_{u}[i,j]=1\right]\quad\forall i,j\in[p],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function, ensuring that an edge <math id="A1.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> exists in the learned graph if and only if it exists in at least one contributing subgraph.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This assumption is well-motivated: when data from multiple causal mechanisms are pooled without knowledge of their source, standard causal discovery algorithms tend to include all statistically supported edges to avoid missing true causal relationships. Violating this assumption would imply that the learning algorithm systematically ignores genuine causal relationships present in the data, leading to incomplete and potentially misleading causal models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F9.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/ground_truth_partition.svg" id="A1.F9.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/336;" width="476" height="336"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F9.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A1.F9.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Causal graph with 3 meta states</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F9.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/ground_truth_matrix.svg" id="A1.F9.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/336;" width="476" height="336"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F9.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A1.F9.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Learned graph with 3 meta states</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F9.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/underparameterization_partition.svg" id="A1.F9.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/336;" width="476" height="336"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F9.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A1.F9.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Causal graph with 2 meta states</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F9.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/underparameterization_matrix.svg" id="A1.F9.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/336;" width="476" height="336"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F9.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A1.F9.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Learned graph with 2 meta states</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F9.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A1.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of the Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The upper figure shows the ground truth causal graph with three meta states and the corresponding causal skeleton matrix. The lower figure shows the learned graph with two meta states and the corresponding causal skeleton matrix. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows that the L1 norm of the learned causal skeleton matrix is larger than the L1 norm of the causal skeleton matrix of the contributing graphs. This also provides a way to show that the underparameterization of meta states will lead to the failure of learning the causal graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A1.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ A.6 Disscussion on Assumption ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis and Discussion ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> shows an example of underparameterization. The upper figure shows the ground truth causal graph with three meta states and the corresponding causal skeleton matrix. The lower figure shows the learned graph with two meta states and the corresponding causal skeleton matrix. The underparameterization of the learned graph leads to the learned causal skeleton matrix different from the ground truth causal skeleton matrix (Equation <a href="#A1.E4" title="In A.6 Disscussion on Assumption ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis and Discussion ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">However, overparameterization does not lead to the failure of learning the causal graph. Figure <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ A.5 Proof of Theorem ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis and Discussion ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> shows an example of overparameterization. Although we may assign different meta states to states which are generated from the same causal graph, the data to learn each causal graph is still generated from the same causal graph.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F10" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23068v1/identify.svg" id="A1.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/168;" width="238" height="168"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A1.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of the Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Mixed Data Structure Learning). ‣ 3.2 Identifiability of Meta States ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The left side shows the causal skeleton matrices of causal graphs which contribute to the dataset for generating the data. The right side shows the learned causal skeleton matrices of the causal graph from the dataset. The elements in red highlight the differences between them.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.7 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (Identifiability of Causal Subgraph). ‣ 3.3.1 Causal Subgraph Identification ‣ 3.3 Identification of Causal Subgraphs ‣ 3 Meta-Causal Graph: Definition and Identifiability ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove that the given intervention condition ensures unique identifiability by showing that no two distinct DAGs can be interventionally Markov equivalent under <math id="A1.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the interventional Markov equivalence class (I-MEC) of DAGs that are interventionally Markov equivalent to <math id="A1.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="A1.SS7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1:<span id="A1.SS7.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Consider any edge <math id="A1.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\to j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\to j</annotation></semantics></math> in the true causal graph <math id="A1.SS7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. By assumption, there exists <math id="A1.SS7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> such that exactly one of <math id="A1.SS7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{i,j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{i,j\}</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="A1.SS7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1:<span id="A1.SS7.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> If <math id="A1.SS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\notin I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\notin I</annotation></semantics></math>, then in the intervention graph <math id="A1.SS7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{(I)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{(I)}</annotation></semantics></math>, all edges into node <math id="A1.SS7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> are removed, but the edge <math id="A1.SS7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\to j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\to j</annotation></semantics></math> (if it exists) remains. Consider any DAG <math id="A1.SS7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the reversed edge <math id="A1.SS7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\to i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\to i</annotation></semantics></math>. In <math id="A1.SS7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime(I)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime(I)}</annotation></semantics></math>, this edge would be removed since <math id="A1.SS7.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in I</annotation></semantics></math>, creating different intervention graphs: <math id="A1.SS7.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{(I)}\neq D^{\prime(I)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{(I)}\neq D^{\prime(I)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:<span id="A1.SS7.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> If <math id="A1.SS7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in I</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\notin I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\notin I</annotation></semantics></math>, then in <math id="A1.SS7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{(I)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{(I)}</annotation></semantics></math>, all edges into <math id="A1.SS7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> (including <math id="A1.SS7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\to j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\to j</annotation></semantics></math>) are removed. A DAG <math id="A1.SS7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with edge <math id="A1.SS7.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\to i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\to i</annotation></semantics></math> would retain this edge in <math id="A1.SS7.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime(I)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime(I)}</annotation></semantics></math> since <math id="A1.SS7.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\notin I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\notin I</annotation></semantics></math>, again yielding <math id="A1.SS7.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{(I)}\neq D^{\prime(I)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≠</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{(I)}\neq D^{\prime(I)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2:<span id="A1.SS7.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Since every edge <math id="A1.SS7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\to j\in D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\to j\in D</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the intervention condition, every edge direction is uniquely determined by the interventional data, ensuring that for any DAG <math id="A1.SS7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}\in\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="A1.SS7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{(I)}=D^{\prime(I)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{(I)}=D^{\prime(I)}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A1.SS7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\in\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\in\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS7.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS7.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3:<span id="A1.SS7.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The intervention condition ensures that any edge orientation different from that in <math id="A1.SS7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> would create distinguishable intervention graphs, contradicting interventional Markov equivalence. Therefore, <math id="A1.SS7.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{I}}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{D}_{\mathcal{I}}|=1</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning <math id="A1.SS7.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is uniquely identifiable.
∎</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.8 </span>Reachability of Interventions</h3>

<div id="A1.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Environmental constraints often prevent direct manipulation of every state variable in practice. We consider a subset <math id="A1.SS8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{c}\subseteq[p]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{c}\subseteq[p]</annotation></semantics></math> of variables permitting direct intervention, while others require multi-step sequences or remain unreachable. We define <em id="A1.SS8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">intervention reachability</em>: a state is reachable if attainable from the current state via finite allowable intervention sequences.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each state variable <math id="A1.SS8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> takes <math id="A1.SS8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> discrete values, represented using one-hot encoding as <math id="A1.SS8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{i}\in\{0,1\}^{n_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{i}\in\{0,1\}^{n_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> where exactly one element equals 1. The complete system state is <math id="A1.SS8.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{z}=\mathbf{z}_{1}\otimes\mathbf{z}_{2}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbf{z}_{p}\in\{0,1\}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=\mathbf{z}_{1}\otimes\mathbf{z}_{2}\otimes\cdots\otimes\mathbf{z}_{p}\in\{0,1\}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\otimes" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Kronecker product and <math id="A1.SS8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\prod_{i=1}^{p}n_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>p</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\prod_{i=1}^{p}n_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Kronecker product of one-hot vectors remains one-hot. If <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{1}\in\{0,1\}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{1}\in\{0,1\}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{2}\in\{0,1\}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{2}\in\{0,1\}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are one-hot vectors, then <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}=\mathbf{z}_{1}\otimes\mathbf{z}_{2}\in\{0,1\}^{mn}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}=\mathbf{z}_{1}\otimes\mathbf{z}_{2}\in\{0,1\}^{mn}</annotation></semantics></math> is also one-hot, with <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}[nr+v]=\mathbf{z}_{1}[r]\mathbf{z}_{2}[v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}[nr+v]=\mathbf{z}_{1}[r]\mathbf{z}_{2}[v]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in[m],v\in[n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in[m],v\in[n]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are one-hot encoded vectors, we have <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{1}[r]\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{1}[r]\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}_{2}[v]\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}_{2}[v]\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Therefore, <math id="Thmremark1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}[nr+v]\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}[nr+v]\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and there exists only one <math id="Thmremark1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nr+v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nr+v</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmremark1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}[nr+v]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}[nr+v]=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus, <math id="Thmremark1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is a one-hot encoded vector.
Therefore, the Kronecker product of one-hot encoded vectors is still a one-hot encoded vector.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Kronecker product <math id="Thmremark1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is a one-hot encoded vector, which can represent the state of the system.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define two key matrices:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intervention Matrix</span> <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\{0,1\}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\{0,1\}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F[i,j]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F[i,j]=1</annotation></semantics></math> if state <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> can be directly reached from state <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> through intervention on variables in <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transition Matrix</span> <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\{0,1\}^{N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\{0,1\}^{N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[i,j]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[i,j]=1</annotation></semantics></math> if the system naturally transitions from intervened state <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to state <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given a state vector <math id="A1.SS8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}\in\{0,1\}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}\in\{0,1\}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, the intervention operation <math id="A1.SS8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{\prime}=F\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{\prime}=F\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> produces:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{\prime}[k]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}F[k,i]\mathbf{z}[i]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{\prime}[k]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}F[k,i]\mathbf{z}[i]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A1.SS8.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> is one-hot, <math id="A1.SS8.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{\prime}[k]&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{\prime}[k]&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there exists an index <math id="A1.SS8.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A1.SS8.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}[i]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}[i]=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS8.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F[k,i]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F[k,i]=1</annotation></semantics></math>. This means state <math id="A1.SS8.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is directly reachable from the current state <math id="A1.SS8.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> through intervention. The non-zero elements of <math id="A1.SS8.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\mathbf{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\mathbf{z}</annotation></semantics></math> thus indicate all states achievable by a single intervention step.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, the transition operation <math id="A1.SS8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}=T\mathbf{z}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}=T\mathbf{z}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> yields:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}[k]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}T[k,i]\mathbf{z}^{\prime}[i]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}[k]=\sum_{i=1}^{N}T[k,i]\mathbf{z}^{\prime}[i]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A1.SS8.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}[k]&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}[k]&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that state <math id="A1.SS8.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> can be reached from some intervened state <math id="A1.SS8.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A1.SS8.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{\prime}[i]&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{\prime}[i]&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS8.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T[k,i]=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T[k,i]=1</annotation></semantics></math>. This captures the natural system dynamics following intervention.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Combining these operations, the complete system evolution under intervention is:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t+1}=TF\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t+1}=TF\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p9.1" class="ltx_p">This composition first applies interventions (via <math id="A1.SS8.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>) to determine immediately accessible states, then applies system dynamics (via <math id="A1.SS8.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>) to find the resulting states after natural transitions. The non-zero elements in <math id="A1.SS8.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> represent all states reachable within <math id="A1.SS8.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> intervention-transition cycles from <math id="A1.SS8.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Reachability Analysis)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A state corresponding to index <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is reachable from initial state <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if there exists <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}][i]&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}][i]&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS8.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS8.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove both directions of the equivalence.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS8.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(<math id="A1.SS8.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>):<span id="A1.SS8.p11.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> If state <math id="A1.SS8.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is reachable from <math id="A1.SS8.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, then by definition there exists a finite sequence of intervention-transition steps that leads from <math id="A1.SS8.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to a state where the <math id="A1.SS8.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th component is active. This sequence corresponds to some power <math id="A1.SS8.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> of the operator <math id="A1.SS8.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TF</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="A1.SS8.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}][i]&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}][i]&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS8.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(<math id="A1.SS8.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇐</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math>):<span id="A1.SS8.p12.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> If <math id="A1.SS8.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}][i]&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}][i]&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A1.SS8.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then by the definition of matrix-vector multiplication, there exists a computational path through <math id="A1.SS8.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> iterations of intervention-transition operations that activates the <math id="A1.SS8.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th state component, demonstrating reachability.
∎</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p13.1" class="ltx_p">The reachability analysis has direct consequences for causal structure learning:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1</span></span><span id="Thmcorollary1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Intervention Feasibility)<span id="Thmcorollary1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The state corresponding to index <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is feasible from initial state <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> by intervention if and only if there exists <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmcorollary1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[F(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}][i]&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[F(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}][i]&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS8.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS8.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows directly from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Reachability Analysis). ‣ A.8 Reachability of Interventions ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis and Discussion ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Here, <math id="A1.SS8.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(TF)^{k}\mathbf{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> represents states accessible for intervention after <math id="A1.SS8.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> cycles, where the leading <math id="A1.SS8.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> captures the final intervention step.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS8.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS8.p15.1" class="ltx_p">This constraint fundamentally limits causal discovery scope in practical settings. The Curious Causality-Seeking Agent must therefore operate within feasible intervention constraints while maximizing causal structure identification. We provide an example to illustrate these results.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmcase1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_case">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmcase1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1</span></span><span id="Thmcase1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmcase1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmcase1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmcase1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a system with two binary variables <math id="Thmcase1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding four possible states as shown in Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Case 1. ‣ A.8 Reachability of Interventions ‣ Appendix A Theoretical Analysis and Discussion ‣ Curious Causality-Seeking Agents Learn Meta Causal World" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="A1.T3.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">One-hot encoding of <math id="A1.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and their Kronecker product.</span></figcaption>
